scenario a good fix goes stale rather than staying good
describe Freshness is decided before quality. An excellent fix from ten minutes
describe ago is not a slightly worse fix: the wearer may have walked anywhere
describe since, so it is a circle whose radius nobody measured.
describe
describe The receiver going quiet is what `hold` means here — no observation
describe arrives, the retained one is re-judged against a later clock, and
describe nothing else about the device changes. That is the only route by which
describe a real device reaches Stale from its own receiver, and until the rig
describe had `hold` this fixture asserted `valid` three times and proved
describe nothing about staleness at all.

at 0
  fix 3d
  pos 5000000 10000000
  alt 42000
  acc 3000
  sats 12 15
  hdop 80
  jam none
  spoof none
  expect validity valid
  expect trust trusted

# Nothing arrives from here on. stale_after is 30 s, so one second short of it
# the answer is still current.
at 29000
  hold
  expect validity valid
  expect no-reason stale-position

# And at it, it is not: the test is `>=`, so thirty seconds exactly is
# already too old.
at 30000
  hold
  expect validity stale
  expect reason stale-position
  expect no-reason fix-lost

at 90000
  hold
  expect validity stale
  expect reason stale-position

# StalePosition weighs 20 against a degrade_at of 30, so age alone does not
# move trust. That is deliberate and it is the two-axis design: validity
# already carries the whole freshness story, and trust is about whether the
# numbers can be believed, not about when they were taken. An interface that
# shows a stale position must say so from the validity, not from the trust
# state.
at 91000
  hold
  expect validity stale
  expect trust trusted

# The receiver comes back with no solution at all. Now there are two different
# things wrong and only one of them is age.
at 120000
  fix none
  sats 0 3
  expect validity nofix
  expect reason fix-lost
  expect no-reason stale-position
